RestTemplate vs Feign Client

Both call other services over HTTP. Style and Spring Cloud integration differ a lot.

────────────────────────────────────────
🔷 RestTemplate (imperative / traditional)

What it is
- Synchronous HTTP client from Spring Web
- You write calls yourself: getForObject, exchange, etc.

Example:

RestTemplate restTemplate = new RestTemplate();
User user = restTemplate.getForObject(
        "http://localhost:8081/users/1",
        User.class
);

Pros
- Simple, direct, full control over request/response
- Fine for monoliths, quick scripts, legacy code

Cons
- Verbose in large microservice codebases
- No declarative interface by default
- In **maintenance mode** — Spring recommends **WebClient** for new non-declarative HTTP clients
  (still available; not deleted, but discouraged for greenfield)

Microservice note:
RestTemplate *can* do discovery/LB with `@LoadBalanced RestTemplate` + service name URL
(`http://user-service/users/1`), but setup is more manual than Feign.

────────────────────────────────────────
🔷 OpenFeign (declarative — Spring Cloud OpenFeign)

What it is
- Declarative REST client
- You declare an interface; Spring Cloud generates the implementation

@FeignClient(name = "user-service")
public interface UserClient {

    @GetMapping("/users/{id}")
    User getUserById(@PathVariable("id") Long id);
}

Usage:

@Autowired
private UserClient userClient;

User user = userClient.getUserById(1L);

Pros
- Clean, readable, easy to maintain
- Works with service discovery (Eureka/Consul/K8s via Spring Cloud)
- Client-side load balancing via Spring Cloud LoadBalancer
- Easy retries/fallback patterns with Resilience4j / circuit breaker integrations

Cons
- Needs Spring Cloud OpenFeign dependency
- Slightly less “raw HTTP” control than RestTemplate/WebClient
- Small learning curve (config, error decoder, timeouts)

────────────────────────────────────────
🔥 Key differences

| Feature            | RestTemplate                      | Feign Client                          |
| ------------------ | --------------------------------- | ------------------------------------- |
| Style              | Imperative                        | Declarative (interface)               |
| Verbosity          | Higher                            | Lower                                 |
| Discovery / LB     | Manual or `@LoadBalanced` bean    | Natural with `name = "service-id"`    |
| Best fit           | Legacy / simple calls             | Spring Cloud microservices            |
| Modern alternative | Prefer **WebClient** for new code | Still preferred for declarative MS calls |
| Complexity         | Simple API                        | Medium (Cloud stack)                  |

────────────────────────────────────────
🚀 When to use what

Use **Feign** when:
- Microservices + Spring Cloud
- Many service-to-service HTTP APIs
- You want interface-based clients

Use **RestTemplate** when:
- Legacy codebase already using it
- One-off / simple sync calls (or migrate to WebClient)

Use **WebClient** when:
- New project needs programmatic HTTP client
- Reactive / non-blocking (or even sync via `.block()` carefully)

────────────────────────────────────────
🔷 WebClient (modern replacement for RestTemplate)

What it is
- Non-blocking, reactive HTTP client from **Spring WebFlux** (`spring-boot-starter-webflux`)
- Spring’s recommended programmatic client instead of RestTemplate
- Can be used in servlet MVC apps too (not only full reactive apps)

Example (async / reactive style):

WebClient client = WebClient.create("http://user-service");

Mono<User> user = client.get()
        .uri("/users/{id}", 1L)
        .retrieve()
        .bodyToMono(User.class);

// in reactive chain: return user;
// in blocking code (use carefully): User u = user.block();

Pros
- Non-blocking I/O → better concurrency under load
- Fluent API, streaming support (bodyToFlux)
- Actively supported; RestTemplate is maintenance-only
- Works with Reactor types: Mono / Flux

Cons
- Learning curve if team only knows sync RestTemplate
- `.block()` in servlet threads can hurt if overused
- Declarative Feign-style interfaces need extra libs (e.g. HTTP interface clients) — WebClient itself is fluent/imperative

RestTemplate vs WebClient vs Feign

| Point        | RestTemplate     | WebClient              | Feign                    |
| ------------ | ---------------- | ---------------------- | ------------------------ |
| Style        | Sync imperative  | Reactive fluent        | Declarative interface    |
| Blocking     | Yes              | No (unless .block())   | Sync by default          |
| Spring stance| Maintenance      | Preferred prog. client | Preferred declarative MS |
| Best for     | Legacy           | New HTTP client code   | Many service APIs / Cloud|

Interview line:
“WebClient is Spring’s modern HTTP client — non-blocking and reactive — recommended instead of RestTemplate for new programmatic calls.
Feign is still great when you want declarative `@FeignClient` interfaces with discovery/LB.”

────────────────────────────────────────
⚠️ “Does Feign have port issues?”

Feign itself does **not** invent port problems.
Issues come from **how URLs / discovery are configured**.

Correct microservice style — service **name**, not host:port:

@FeignClient(name = "user-service")

Registry + LoadBalancer resolve instance IP/port for you.

When people hit “port issues”:

1. Hardcoded URL
   `@FeignClient(url = "http://localhost:8081")`
   → breaks when port/env/Docker mapping changes

2. Service not registered / wrong name
   → UnknownHostException / connection errors (discovery problem, not Feign bug)

3. Docker/K8s networking wrong
   → calling host port instead of service DNS / cluster IP

4. Stale registry instance
   → LB routes to a dead instance (registry health / TTL issue)

Interview line:
“Feign doesn’t cause port issues. Hardcoded urls or misconfigured discovery/LB/container ports do.
With `@FeignClient(name = \"user-service\")`, ports are resolved via the registry and load balancer.”

| Port handling        | RestTemplate (raw URL) | Feign (service name) |
| -------------------- | ---------------------- | -------------------- |
| Who picks port?      | You                    | Discovery + LB       |
| Hardcode risk        | High if URL fixed      | Low if name-based    |
| MS-friendly default  | Needs extra setup      | Yes                  |

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ Short Interview Answer

RestTemplate is an imperative sync HTTP client — flexible but verbose; Spring prefers WebClient for new code.
Feign is a declarative client: define an interface with `@FeignClient`, Spring implements it and integrates with service discovery and load balancing.
For Spring Cloud microservices, Feign (or similar declarative clients) keeps inter-service calls cleaner than hand-written RestTemplate calls.
